@using Booster.Licensing.Portal.Services
<div class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
        <p class="kicker reveal">Self-hosted identity and access management</p>
        <h1 class="reveal d2">Your identity data stays on <em>your</em> servers.</h1>
        <div class="hero-grid">
            <div>
                <p class="lede reveal d3">Booster Auth is an OpenID Connect provider that runs sign-in, permissions and sessions for your employees and customers
                    inside your own infrastructure. One script installs it, it ships with secure defaults, and even the license is verified offline.</p>
                <div class="cta-row reveal d3">
                    <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/en/download">Try it free for 7 days <span class="arr" aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
                    <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="#security">Review the security model</a>
                </div>
                <p class="cta-note">The install package is about 15 KB. A company e-mail address is all you need for the trial.</p>
            </div>
            <aside class="hero-aside reveal d3" aria-label="Installation in short">
                <p class="q">One zip file, one script, one command.</p>
                <p>The setup script asks a few questions and generates every password and key on your own server. Docker Compose does the rest.</p>
                <ol class="quick">
                    <li><span>Get</span><code>booster-auth.zip</code></li>
                    <li><span>Prepare</span><code>./setup.sh</code></li>
                    <li><span>Start</span><code>docker compose up -d</code></li>
                    <li><span>Sign in</span><code>localhost:3000 · admin</code></li>
                </ol>
            </aside>
        </div>
        <dl class="ledger">
            <div><dt>Runs on</dt><dd>Your own servers, with Docker Compose</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Protocol</dt><dd>OpenID Connect and OAuth 2.0</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Corporate directory</dt><dd>Sign in with LDAP / Active Directory</dd></div>
            <div><dt>License check</dt><dd>Offline; never calls a license server</dd></div>
        </dl>
    </div>
</div>

<section id="product" class="sec-rule" style="margin-top:96px">
    <div class="wrap">
        <div class="sec-head reveal">
            <div>
                <p class="kicker">Why self-hosted</p>
                <h2>Identity is the last layer you should hand to someone else.</h2>
            </div>
            <p>Cloud identity services get you started quickly, but where user data lives, whether sign-in keeps working and what it costs remain the
                provider's decisions. Booster Auth runs the same standards on your servers, under your rules.</p>
        </div>
        <table class="compare reveal">
            <caption>The questions that decide it</caption>
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Question</th><th scope="col">Cloud identity service</th><th scope="col" class="us">Booster Auth</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><th scope="row">Where is user data stored?</th><td data-h="Cloud service">In the region and data centre the provider chooses.</td><td class="us" data-h="Booster Auth">On your server, in a PostgreSQL database you back up.</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Does sign-in survive losing the outside connection?</th><td data-h="Cloud service">It needs access to the service.</td><td class="us" data-h="Booster Auth">Yes. Sign-in happens inside your network, and licensing never calls out either.</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">How is the license checked?</th><td data-h="Cloud service">The subscription is managed on the provider's side.</td><td class="us" data-h="Booster Auth">A signed license key is verified in your installation; there is no contact with a license server at all.</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">What happens when the license expires?</th><td data-h="Cloud service">It depends on the contract.</td><td class="us" data-h="Booster Auth">With a paid license, end-user sign-ins never stop. The admin console turns read-only after a 14-day grace period.</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">What makes the cost grow?</th><td data-h="Cloud service">Usually the number of active users, every month.</td><td class="us" data-h="Booster Auth">An annual plan per installation. No per-user monthly fee.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
</section>

<section id="architecture" class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
        <div class="sec-head reveal">
            <div>
                <p class="kicker">Architecture</p>
                <h2>Inside your network, in front of your applications.</h2>
            </div>
            <p>Your applications talk to Booster Auth over standard OpenID Connect; any OIDC library will do. The database, cache, message broker and
                authorization engine stay on the internal network and are never exposed.</p>
        </div>
        <figure class="arch-fig reveal">
            <p class="scroll-hint">Scroll sideways to see the whole diagram →</p>
            <div class="arch-scroll" tabindex="0" aria-label="Architecture diagram, scrolls horizontally">
                @SiteArt.Architecture("en")
            </div>
            <figcaption>Simplified diagram. External providers and the corporate directory are contacted only if you configure them. Only the HTTPS
                gateway and the admin console are reachable from outside.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <div class="arch-below">
            <div class="reveal">
                <p class="kicker">Structure</p>
                <h3>Customers and employees in one system, under separate rules.</h3>
                <p class="prose-2">Inside each tenant you define isolated user pools. Every pool has its own sign-in, password, MFA, session, registration and
                    redirect policies. Passwordless e-mail codes for your customer portal and Active Directory sign-in with SMS two-factor for your staff
                    run side by side in the same installation.</p>
                <p class="prose-2" style="margin:0">Authorization goes beyond roles and scopes: with a relationship schema built on OpenFGA you ask
                    “can this user edit this document?” in a single API call.</p>
            </div>
            <ul class="tree reveal" aria-label="Example tenant structure">
                <li><span class="node t"><b>Tenant</b><i>Northwind Group</i></span>
                    <ul>
                        <li><span class="node p"><b>Pool · Customers</b><i>passwordless e-mail code</i></span>
                            <ul><li><div class="leaf-row"><span class="leaf">Applications</span><span class="leaf">Users</span><span class="leaf">Roles and scopes</span></div></li></ul>
                        </li>
                        <li><span class="node p"><b>Pool · Employees</b><i>Active Directory + SMS 2FA</i></span>
                            <ul><li><div class="leaf-row"><span class="leaf">Applications</span><span class="leaf">Users</span><span class="leaf">Service accounts</span><span class="leaf">Authorization schema</span></div></li></ul>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section id="security" aria-labelledby="sec">
    <div class="wrap">
        <div class="sec-head reveal">
            <div>
                <p class="kicker">Security</p>
                <h2 id="sec">Secure defaults come with the install.</h2>
            </div>
            <p>Hardening is not a checklist for after go-live. Every service in the package starts with restricted privileges, secrets are generated only
                on your server, and weak protocol options are simply not offered.</p>
        </div>
        <dl class="spec reveal">
            <div><dt>Hardened services<small>read_only · non-root · cap_drop</small></dt>
                <dd>Services run with read-only filesystems, as non-root users, with Linux capabilities dropped and <code>no-new-privileges</code> set.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Small network surface<small>HTTPS · console</small></dt>
                <dd>Only the HTTPS gateway and the admin console are exposed. PostgreSQL, Redis, Kafka and OpenFGA stay on the internal network.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Your secrets stay yours<small>.env</small></dt>
                <dd>The setup script generates every password and key at random; none of them is shared with us. If any value in <code>.env</code> is
                    still <code>CHANGE_ME</code>, the system refuses to start and names the variable.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Password storage<small>Argon2 · pepper</small></dt>
                <dd>Passwords are hashed with peppered Argon2; sensitive data is encrypted with keys that exist only in your <code>.env</code> file.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Strict protocol<small>PKCE S256 · RS256</small></dt>
                <dd>PKCE (S256) is mandatory, there is no implicit flow and tokens are signed with RS256. Redirect URIs must match exactly, and each pool
                    can restrict them to an allow-list of domains.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Account protection<small>lockout · policy · 2FA</small></dt>
                <dd>Accounts lock after repeated failed attempts. The password policy covers length, character classes, expiry and history; two-factor
                    with e-mail or SMS codes can be required per pool.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Sessions and consent<small>revoke · invite</small></dt>
                <dd>A consent screen can be shown per application; sessions and granted consents can be revoked from the console. Invitation links
                    are single-use and valid for one day.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Privacy-minded audit trail<small>who · when · from where</small></dt>
                <dd>Sign-ins, failed attempts, account changes, every change made from the console and reads of screens with personal data go to a separate
                    audit stream. Entries hold the user ID, IP and outcome; e-mail addresses, phone numbers, names, passwords, codes and tokens are never
                    written. E-mails, phone numbers and tokens that might slip into operational logs are masked before they leave the service.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Logs and monitoring on your network<small>OpenTelemetry · OpenObserve</small></dt>
                <dd>Logs, traces and metrics go to the bundled OpenObserve; its UI is reachable only from your local network and nothing is sent to us.
                    Sent e-mail and SMS records are stored encrypted and deleted after 30 days. You can point everything at your own OTLP system instead.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>HTTPS out of the box<small>local CA</small></dt>
                <dd>A local certificate authority and HTTPS certificate are generated on first start. In production you bring your own certificate or
                    reverse proxy.</dd></div>
        </dl>
    </div>
</section>

<section id="features" class="sec-rule" aria-labelledby="cap">
    <div class="wrap">
        <div class="sec-head reveal">
            <div>
                <p class="kicker">Capabilities</p>
                <h2 id="cap">What you expect from an identity platform, in one package.</h2>
            </div>
            <p>Every feature is in every plan. Plans differ only by their user, pool and application limits.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="caps">
            <article class="cap reveal">
                <div><h3>Protocol</h3><p class="lab">OIDC · OAuth 2.0</p></div>
                <p>A standards-compliant provider. You connect existing applications with the OIDC library you already use, not a proprietary SDK.</p>
                <ul><li>Authorization Code + PKCE</li><li>Refresh tokens</li><li>Token exchange</li><li>Service accounts <span>(client credentials)</span></li><li>One issuer for every pool</li></ul>
            </article>
            <article class="cap reveal">
                <div><h3>Sign-in</h3><p class="lab">Policy per pool</p></div>
                <p>Sign-in methods and rules that match the risk profile of each audience.</p>
                <ul><li>Password sign-in</li><li>Passwordless e-mail / SMS codes</li><li>Two-factor via e-mail / SMS</li><li>Password reset</li><li>Password policy <span>(length, classes, expiry, history)</span></li><li>Account lockout</li></ul>
            </article>
            <article class="cap reveal">
                <div><h3>Corporate directory</h3><p class="lab">LDAP · Active Directory</p></div>
                <p>Employees sign in on the normal form with their directory username and password. Passwords stay in the directory; the account is created on first sign-in.</p>
                <ul><li>LDAPS or StartTLS</li><li>Service account bind</li><li>Search base and filter</li><li>Attribute mapping</li><li>Connection test</li><li>Name and e-mail refreshed on every sign-in</li></ul>
            </article>
            <article class="cap reveal">
                <div><h3>External providers</h3><p class="lab">Federation</p></div>
                <p>Users sign in with the account they already have; the identity record stays in your database.</p>
                <ul><li>Google, Microsoft, Apple</li><li>LinkedIn, Facebook, GitHub, X</li><li>Any OpenID Connect provider</li><li>SAML</li></ul>
            </article>
            <article class="cap reveal">
                <div><h3>Authorization</h3><p class="lab">Role · scope · relationship</p></div>
                <p>From simple role checks to relationship-based permissions on individual records.</p>
                <ul><li>Roles and scopes</li><li>OpenFGA relationship schema <span>(draft / live)</span></li><li>Relationship records</li><li>Single and batch checks</li><li>Access check in the console</li></ul>
            </article>
            <article class="cap reveal">
                <div><h3>Administration</h3><p class="lab">Delegation</p></div>
                <p>Each team manages its own area; the central team sees the whole installation.</p>
                <ul><li>Owner / Manager / Viewer</li><li>On tenants, pools and applications</li><li>Add users with e-mail invitations</li><li>CSV import <span>(up to 1000 rows)</span></li><li>Revoke sessions and consents</li><li>English / Turkish interface</li></ul>
                <p class="more"><a class="text-link" href="/en/features">See every feature →</a></p>
            </article>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="band-2" aria-labelledby="console">
    <div class="wrap console-grid">
        <div class="reveal">
            <p class="kicker">Admin console</p>
            <h2 id="console">Built for everyday work.</h2>
            <p class="prose-2" style="margin-top:20px">The help desk signs a user out, an application team configures its own client, the security team withdraws a consent.
                Everyone sees only the area they are allowed to manage.</p>
            <ul class="notes">
                <li><b>Users and invitations</b>Add users one by one and invite them, or import up to 1000 rows from CSV.</li>
                <li><b>Sessions and consents</b>See a user's active sessions and application consents, and revoke them.</li>
                <li><b>License and usage</b>Track usage against your plan limits and the expiry date in the console.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="reveal d2"><partial name="~/Views/Site/_ConsoleMock.cshtml" /></div>
    </div>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="licensing">
    <div class="wrap lic-grid">
        <div class="reveal">
            <p class="kicker">Licensing</p>
            <h2 id="licensing">Your license never locks your users out.</h2>
            <p class="prose-2">The license key is verified locally in your installation. Booster Auth never connects to a license server, so it works the
                same way on networks with no internet access.</p>
            <ul class="terms">
                <li><b>Offline</b><span>Paste the key into the console; no service restart needed.</span></li>
                <li><b>Early renewal</b><span>You lose nothing: the new term is added to the current end date.</span></li>
                <li><b>Server moves</b><span>Transfer the license to a new installation yourself, up to 3 times a year.</span></li>
                <li><b>Reminders</b><span>E-mails 30 and 7 days before expiry.</span></li>
            </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="timeline reveal d2">
            <h3>When a paid license expires</h3>
            <p class="sub">Only administration is restricted, and only after 14 days.</p>
            @SiteArt.Timeline("en")
            <div class="legend"><span><i style="background:#2F6B4F"></i>Sign-ins</span><span><i style="background:#173F38"></i>Full administration</span><span><i style="background:#9C5E25;opacity:.75"></i>Grace period</span><span><i style="background:#D6CEBF"></i>Read-only</span></div>
            <p class="fine">Trial licenses have a @LicenseIssuer.Defaults.TrialGraceDays-day grace period, after which end-user sign-ins stop as well.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

