<section aria-labelledby="faq" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="wrap faq-grid">
        <div class="reveal">
            <p class="kicker">FAQ</p>
            <h2 id="faq">Questions we hear during evaluations.</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="faq reveal">
            <details open><summary>How long does installation take?</summary><p>Running <code>./setup.sh</code> takes as long as answering a few prompts. Then
                <code>docker compose up -d</code> pulls the images and starts the services; how long that takes depends mostly on your connection. There
                are no passwords or keys to fill in by hand.</p></details>
            <details><summary>Can our Active Directory users sign in?</summary><p>Yes. On the pool's Identity Providers screen you define the LDAP /
                Active Directory connection (server, LDAPS / StartTLS, service account, search base and filter, attribute mapping) and test it. Users sign
                in on the normal form with their directory username and password; the account is created on first sign-in and name and e-mail are
                refreshed from the directory each time. Passwords stay in the directory.</p></details>
            <details><summary>Does it work on a network with no internet access?</summary><p>As far as licensing goes, yes: Booster Auth verifies its license
                offline and never contacts a license server. Sign-in happens entirely inside your network. Internet access is only needed to pull images
                during installation, and external identity providers (e.g. Google, Microsoft) are used only if you configure them.</p></details>
            <details><summary>How do we connect our existing applications?</summary><p>Booster Auth is a standard OpenID Connect / OAuth 2.0 provider.
                Configure the Authorization Code + PKCE flow with any OIDC library for your language. Service-to-service calls use service accounts.
                See the <a class="text-link" href="/en/docs/integration">integration guide</a>.</p></details>
            <details><summary>Are our users affected if the license expires?</summary><p>Not with a paid license. End-user sign-ins continue. The admin
                console becomes read-only after a @LicenseIssuer.Defaults.PaidGraceDays-day grace period, and renewing restores full access.</p></details>
            <details><summary>What happens to the license if we move servers?</summary><p>You transfer the license to your new installation yourself in
                the license portal, up to 3 times a year.</p></details>
            <details><summary>How are logs kept with personal data protection in mind?</summary><p>Data stays on your server; logs and audit records go to the
                bundled OpenObserve and nothing is sent to us. Audit records show who accessed personal data, when and from where; they hold the user ID and IP,
                never e-mail addresses, phone numbers, names, passwords, codes or tokens. Those values are masked in operational logs, and sent e-mail / SMS
                records are stored encrypted and deleted after 30 days. You set retention periods according to your own retention policy. As the data
                controller, privacy notices and policies remain yours. See the <a class="text-link" href="/en/docs/operations#logs">operations guide</a>.</p></details>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

